title: The Single Gospel Through-Line in American Evangelicalism
description: A brief outline following one gospel of grace through faith alone from the Great Awakenings through Moody and Billy Sunday, the fracturing period, and its preservation in the Independent Fundamental Baptist tradition.
output: lessons/history/single-gospel-through-line.html

# Built from the brief outline studies/data/notes/single-gospel-through-line-notes.html, in its order and under its own headings.
# The quoted gospel words of Acts 16:31 are pulled from the KJV by the builder.

=== Introduction
--- The Single Gospel Through-Line [title]
overline: Baptist & church history
heading: The Single Gospel Through-Line in American Evangel­icalism
sub: And Its Preservation in the IFB Tradition
refs: Acts 16:31
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

=== 1. Colonial & Early Republic Era (1740s–1830s)
--- The Great Awakenings
step: 1730s–40s | **First Great Awakening:** Jonathan Edwards, George Whitefield, and Tennent brothers preach salvation by free grace through faith alone; instantaneous justification the moment a sinner trusts Christ.
step: 1790s–1830s | **Second Great Awakening:** Though methods vary (camp meetings, “new measures”), the core message across Presbyterians, Baptists, and most Methodists remains the same: man is lost, Christ’s blood is the only ground of pardon, believe and be saved now and forever.

--- Believe, and be saved
center: → The gospel is overwhelmingly
big: Acts 16:31 | mark: Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and thou shalt be saved
center: (no works, no second blessing, no Sabbath, no tongues required).

=== 2. Mid-19th Century Consensus (1840–1880)
--- One gospel
lead: Revivalists (Finney, Phoebe Palmer, etc.) introduce emotional intensity and sometimes questionable methods, but mainstream evangelical pulpits (Baptist, Presbyterian, Congregational, and most Methodist) still proclaim one gospel: justification by faith alone, received in a moment.
callout: Eternal security is debated (Calvinists vs. many Methodists), but the ground and instrument of salvation are not: Christ’s finished work + faith = eternal life.

=== 3. Late 19th–Early 20th Century (1880–1930)
--- The high-water mark
lead: D. L. Moody, R. A. Torrey, and Billy Sunday carry the exact same “believe and be saved” message to millions in urban crusades.
p: No second blessing, no tongues, no investigative judgment, no loss of salvation taught as part of the gospel offer.
callout: This is the high-water mark of broad evangelical unity on the gospel itself.

=== 4. Fracturing Period (1890–1950)
--- Additions and erosion
panel-warn: Radical Holiness → Pentecostalism (1901–1916) | Adds “second work of grace” and tongues as normative or required.
panel-warn: Seventh-day Adventism & Jehovah’s Witnesses | Arise with new mediators/authorities and works-righteousness elements.
panel-warn: Mainline liberalism | Mainline liberalism and neo-evangelical compromise begin eroding the old landmarks.

=== 5. Fundamentalist & Independent Baptist Response (1920s–present)
--- Refusing the additions and subtractions
lead: Fundamentalists (and especially the independent Baptist stream—J. Frank Norris, John R. Rice, Sword of the Lord, Jack Hyles, etc.) deliberately refuse the new additions and subtractions.

--- The pre-1890 evangelical gospel
p: They consciously preserve the pre-1890 evangelical gospel:
- Salvation is by grace alone through faith alone in Christ alone
- It is received in a moment, the instant one believes
- It is eternal (the believer possesses everlasting life right now)
- Nothing else (tongues, Sabbath, perfection, sacraments, lordship commitment, etc.) is part of the saving transaction.

=== Conclusion & Through-Line
--- Unchanged
lead: From Whitefield in 1740 to Moody in 1880 to Billy Sunday in 1915 to John R. Rice in 1950 to a faithful Independent Fundamental Baptist preacher in 2025, the gospel invitation has remained unchanged:
step: 1740 | Whitefield
step: 1880 | Moody
step: 1915 | Billy Sunday
step: 1950 | John R. Rice
step: 2025 | A faithful Independent Fundamental Baptist preacher

--- The gospel invitation [title]
overline: Baptist & church history
quote: “Christ died for your sins, was buried, and rose again. Believe on Him alone right now and you have everlasting life—freely, forever, and without fail.”
lead: That is the single unbroken thread that the historic IFB movement still holds high today.
